## 7. Registration system

Participants must not need an application account to register.

Required fields:

- Full name
- Email address
- University or institution
- Specialization

Requirements:

- One registration per normalized email address per event.
- No arbitrary registration cap.
- No external Google Form.
- No email confirmation workflow.
- Show an in-app confirmation only after the server confirms successful registration.
- Prevent duplicate registrations using trusted server-side logic and appropriate database constraints or transaction-based enforcement.
- Validate input on both client and server.
- Normalize email addresses consistently.
- Apply sensible rate limiting and anti-spam protection.
- Use CAPTCHA or another suitable challenge when suspicious behavior requires it.
- Never expose registration lists or personal data to public visitors.
- Require a mandatory privacy-consent checkbox before submission.
- Link to a clear privacy notice describing collected data, purpose, access, and retention.
- Do not collect unnecessary personal information.

Registration management:

- Authorized Registration Managers and Super Admins can search and view registrations.
- Registration details can be changed by an authorized organizer only after identity verification.
- Support CSV export for authorized roles only.
- Record sensitive operations in the audit log.
- Protect exports against unauthorized access and accidental exposure.
- Do not track actual attendance.
- Do not implement QR check-in.

### Data retention

Registration data must be scheduled for deletion 180 days after the associated event ends.

Implement this using trusted server-side scheduled logic where supported.

Requirements:

- Use a verified event end date.
- Do not delete data if the event end date is missing or unverified.
- Log deletion outcomes without unnecessarily retaining deleted personal information.
- Provide observable failure reporting and a way for an authorized administrator to verify execution.
- Account for backups and logs so personal data is not retained unnecessarily beyond the intended retention period.
- Test deletion using disposable test records before production use.

If automatic deletion requires a paid Firebase plan, do not activate it without approval. Explain the limitation and provide a safe, documented alternative for the project owner to approve.

## 8. Admin dashboard and permissions

Create a separate, protected admin interface with responsive layouts.

### Super Admin

May:

- Manage authorized organizer accounts and roles.
- Create, edit, review, publish, and archive content.
- Manage events, speakers, resources, translations, and official links.
- View and manage registrations.
- Edit participant registration details after identity verification.
- Export registration data.
- Review audit records within the role's permitted scope.
- Manage beta feedback and bug reports.
- Approve production readiness and initiate the release process.

Require two-step verification for the Super Admin's Google account where available.

### Editor

May:

- Create and edit content.
- Submit content for review.
- View content review status.
- Publish urgent announcements directly, with mandatory audit logging.

Must not access participant registration data or registration exports.

### Registration Manager

May:

- View and search registrations.
- Update registration details after identity verification.
- Export registration data to CSV.
- View audit records relevant to permitted registration operations.

Must not manage organizer roles or publish general content unless separately authorized.

### Security enforcement

- Use individual Google accounts for organizers.
- Only allow explicitly authorized accounts.
- Do not use a shared team password.
- Restrict account and role management to Super Admin.
- Remove access promptly when an organizer leaves the team.
- Enforce every permission in trusted backend logic and Firebase Security Rules.
- Never rely only on hiding buttons or routes in the UI.
- Deny access by default.
- Protect against unauthorized document reads, writes, exports, role escalation, and direct API requests.
- Verify the caller's identity and role on sensitive operations.
- Never place privileged service-account credentials in the browser.

If an authorization check fails, deny the action and show an appropriate error.

## 9. Content management and review workflow

